+# GOAL — block 10: the recorded-set Gibbs theorem — every formation law is a Gibbs law whose Markov graph is the recorded-set graph (launched 2026-09-15)
+
+Branch `physics-loop/admissibility-induced-law-block10-recorded-set-gibbs-theorem-20260915`, cut from block 09's tip; stacked PR (base = block 09's branch). The note uses block 08's product-form regrouping and the third-difference criterion, block 09's L2 for the `Z^3` corollary, and block 01's Theorem B for the equality case; the finite-window theorem itself is self-contained. Supervisor-run seats. Control first: `specs/supervisor_control_block10_recorded_set.py` (+ `.out.txt`).
+
+## Exact target contract
+
+| field | content |
+|---|---|
+| Target statement | For the six-axis product rule under the records-only reading, on any finite window `W` (a finite induced subgraph of `Z^3`) and any total order `σ` with recorded sets `A_x` (the neighbors of `x` that formed earlier): (T1) the formation law is `μ_σ(v) = Π_{x: A_x = ∅} (1/6) · Π_{edges} K(v_x, v_y) / Π_{x: |A_x| ≥ 2} K_{|A_x|}(v_{A_x})` — a Gibbs law for the potential with `−log K` on every edge of `W` and `+log K_k` on every recorded set of size `k ≥ 2`; (T2) `μ_σ` is a Markov field for the recorded-set graph `G_σ = E(W) ∪ ⋃_x {pairs inside A_x}`; (T3) the canonical (vacuum-normalized) potential of `μ_σ` — unique for a positive law — has a nonzero term on every maximal recorded set `A` of size `k ≥ 2` whose `k`-th mixed difference of `log K_k` is nonzero (executed nonzero for `k = 2, …, 6` at `(3,1,2)` and `(5,2,4)`; zero at the constant rule), and the pairs inside such a set are never adjacent (the lattice is bipartite); hence (T4) `μ_σ` is a nearest-neighbor Markov field if and only if every recorded set has at most one element — block 01's condition, now for the Markov property rather than for equality with the static law — and when it fails `μ_σ` is not Markov for any graph missing a pair of co-recorded neighbors; (T5) the induced interaction has range `√2` and `2` (orthogonal and opposite co-recorded neighbors) and genuine `k`-body terms up to `k = 6`; (T6) executed canonical potentials: the plaquette with its last site recording two (pair term `12/13` on the co-recorded non-adjacent pair, zero on the other non-edge on all 1296 configurations) and the star with its center last (three-body term on the leaves). (T7) On `Z^3`, for the monotone class in block 08's region, block 09's L2 turns T4 into the infinite-volume statement already proved there; for other order classes the infinite-volume law is not constructed. |
+| Quantifiers / domain | every finite window and every total order for T1–T2 and the structure of T3–T5; the nonzero mixed differences executed at two triples for `k ≤ 6` (every recorded set has at most six elements); the two executed windows for T6 |
+| Allowed premises | `minimal_axioms`; the supplied readings of blocks 01–05, 08, 09; block 01's Theorem B and definition of the formation law; block 08's Q1e–f; block 09's L2 |
+| Forbidden weakenings | no physical order; no static-law statement beyond block 01's equality case; no infinite-volume claim for non-monotone classes |
+| Required edge cases | the constant rule (all terms vanish; independent uniform law); a window and order with every `|A_x| ≤ 1` (a tree swept from a leaf; `μ_σ` = the static law, block 01); recorded sets that are contained in others (only maximal ones are certified) |
+| Completion witness | the finite-window proof of T1–T5 with the canonical-potential uniqueness re-proved; the exact mixed-difference ratios for `k = 2..6`; the plaquette and star canonical potentials |
+| Outcomes that do not count | a restatement of block 01's Theorem B; a Markov statement without the canonical-potential argument |
